Kevin Wayne wrote:
> It was inscribed by Boudewijn Waijers in the annals dated 2/14/05:

>> After you've gained the protection, the racket ends. Hence, Byron was
>> most probably talking about the playing style *afterwards*.

> Well, yes, of course. However, focusing on the racket as a strategy is
> *training* oneself to play with a particular style.

In my case, it isn't. I use the protection racket only to easily gain
the extra points of protection, and only when the RNG gives me either a
tourist, healer, or gnome to start with.

> I think most proponents of the racket are also proponents of keeping
> one's level relatively low throughout the game.

Since you say "most", you might be correct, I cannot judge that. But I
myself don't try to keep my level low: I take the opportunities the game
gives me by eating wraiths and quaffing non-cursed potions of gain
level.

> I don't suspect
> that most protection-racketeers completely change up once they've
> gotten the added protection, especially since 1) Minetown at level 1
> is still a dangerous place, and 2) some of the alleged benefits of
> the racket still obtain even after the protection bonus--particularly
> lower-level monster generation.

Well, I do change up completely. I see the benefits of the protection
racket, but once these are gained, I immediately retreat to the main
dungeon, meanwhile slaying some gnomes that have remained in the Mines
on the way up. Once I'm safely back, I play my character as if I just
started it.

>> A single healer of mine actually reached -1 naked AC once, but I
>> consider that exceptional.

> Exactly. That would be 11 points of protection. And since you have a
> very methodical playing style, I would expect you to have had more
> success at that than most people. Extravagant claims beyond that
> point I consider spurious.

Well, one could be *very, very* lucky once and get three more points,
but that would only happen in cases where you'd played and succeeded at
a thousand protection racket characters, on average.

> Yes, healers seem to be cut out for this strategy, although my most
> successful healer (see sig) didn't happen to use it. Just slowly
> advanced without any early wishes or special luck. Would've been quite
> ascendable if it weren't for YASD.

